\chapter{Views and Menus in WPF}\label{ch:win-views}

This chapter is the Windows counterpart of Chapters~\ref{ch:views} and~\ref{ch:commands}. The
nine files in [[Windows/LineScope.App/Views]] mirror the SwiftUI views one by one: the document
window with its zones, panes and tabs, the image canvas where the line is drawn, the two charts,
and the three auxiliary windows (the parameter dialog, the test-pattern generator and the
inspector). Their \emph{behavior} is meant to be identical, and where it is, this chapter only
points back to the Swift chapter that explains it. The prose concentrates on what the port had to
change, and why.

Three changes run through the whole chapter.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Views are built in C\#, without XAML.} Each view is a class deriving from a WPF
  element ([[Window]], [[DockPanel]], [[ScrollViewer]], [[FrameworkElement]]) whose constructor
  creates its children. The code then reads much like the SwiftUI [[body]] it replaces, with the
  same names and the same numbers, and a reader can compare the two side by side. The only XAML in
  the application is [[App.xaml]] (Chapter~\ref{ch:win-overview}).
\item \textbf{Redrawing is explicit.} SwiftUI tracks which observable properties a [[body]] reads
  and redraws exactly those views. WPF has no such mechanism for plain C\# objects. So the
  [[Session]] of Chapter~\ref{ch:win-model} raises one event, [[Changed]], with a set of
  [[SessionChange]] flags ([[Line]], [[Settings]], [[Variants]], [[Panes]], [[Focus]],
  [[Running]]), and every view decides from the flags what it has to update. A line drag, the most
  frequent change by far, then redraws the line overlays and the charts and nothing else.
\item \textbf{Menus belong to windows.} A Windows application has no global menu bar. Each
  document window carries its own, bound to its own session, which removes the whole question
  of Chapter~\ref{ch:commands} of which document a menu acts on.
\end{itemize}

