@ In WPF a menu item's [[InputGestureText]] is only a label; it does not make the shortcut work.
So [[Add]] registers the same command twice: on the menu item, and as a [[KeyBinding]] on the
window. Because the binding belongs to the window, the shortcut acts on this window's session and
only while this window is active, which is exactly the scope of its menu. The label is generated
from the gesture, in the user's language.

<<Win MainWindow: adding a menu item and its shortcut>>=
private static MenuItem TopMenu(string header) => new() { Header = header };

private void Add(MenuItem parent, string header, Action action, Func<bool>? canExecute = null, KeyGesture? key = null,
    string? gestureText = null)
{
    var command = new RelayCommand(action, canExecute);
    var item = new MenuItem { Header = header, Command = command };
    if (key is not null)
    {
        InputBindings.Add(new KeyBinding(command, key));
        item.InputGestureText = key.GetDisplayStringForCulture(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture);
    }
    else if (gestureText is not null)
    {
        item.InputGestureText = gestureText;
    }
    parent.Items.Add(item);
}
@ SwiftUI presents the parameter sheet in reaction to the session's [[pendingRequest]]. A modal
WPF dialog is simpler: [[ShowDialog]] returns only when the dialog is closed, so the request is a
local value, and the operation built by the dialog runs right after it. The session needs no
pending-request state at all.

<<Win MainWindow: running and requesting operations>>=
private void Perform(FilterKind filter) => _session?.Perform(new ImageOperation.Filter(filter));

private void Request(OperationRequest request)
{
    if (_session is null) return;
    var dialog = new ParameterDialog(request, _session) { Owner = this };
    if (dialog.ShowDialog() == true && dialog.Operation is { } op) _session.Perform(op);
}
@ \section{Zones}\label{sec:win-views-zones}

A zone is laid out as in Chapter~\ref{ch:views}: a header bar with the title on the left, the
split buttons on the right and the zone's own controls next to them, above one or more panes side
by side. The difference is in how it stays current. SwiftUI rebuilds [[ZoneView.body]] whenever
something it reads changes; the WPF zone is built once and then patched by [[Update]].

<<Windows/LineScope.App/Views/ZoneView.cs>>=
